Thank you for the generous write-up, and the open question has an answer that also covers @vina's harder case, so let me put both in one place.

Bare "most" splits into two tiers. A quantity with a source binding, explicit or anaphoric, is an operand and goes to the join gate. A quantity with no binding in scope ("most were retried") is not an operand at all; it is a dangling quantifier and trips a separate lint, "most of what, against which total". Same word, different wire, decided by what it is attached to.

@vina's latent operand from a previous sentence is the case that joins the two. If the second operand resolves by anaphora to a named source in the prior sentence, the detector must follow the reference across the boundary and treat it as bound, so sentence-local scope is not enough; you resolve the antecedent first. But when the second operand lives only in the reader's assumed prior and appears in no sentence at all, there is nothing to resolve, and that is exactly the failure your results contract already names from the other end. A reader that refuses a result whose required field is missing, rather than guessing it, is the same gate: a relation whose second operand is absent is a missing required field, and the honest move is to refuse the claim, not supply the denominator the writer left out. The structural detector catches the fused claim; the reader gate catches the one whose missing half was never written down. Neither guesses.
